{"ok": true, "summary": "Cause: the typed build stored one evaluated default value in AxM and cloned it for each missing defaultdict key. For mutable defaults (dict/list/tuple containers) that clone shares the same object (AxRow/handles), so all keys saw one shared value instead of CPython re-running the lambda. Additionally, `times, errs = data[path]` unpacking did not bind the stored entry's items because the map's full value type was unknown when a tuple slot started empty.

Change: AxM now stores an `Arc<dyn Fn() -> V + Send + Sync>` constructor for mutable defaults (`ax_lax_mk`), run per missing key, while immutable defaults still use the stored-value `ax_lax_def`. The parser lowers `defaultdict(lambda: ...)` to `lax_mk` for literal container defaults (closed, no names), and the guard mirrors this (immutable vs fresh). Emit/infer/pybridge know `lax_mk`. For unpacking reads of tuple-shaped defaultdict entries, `dd_arities` records the factory's tuple arity and `dd_unpack_shape` lets `dd_unpack` fire before the full value type is known; loop hoisting skips those names so the reference bindings compile. `default_reads` now refuses `is`/`is not` comparisons of two defaultdict entries (typed values have no identity), so `d["x"] is d["y"]` prints False via the dynamic build.

Tested: build ok; run_check exit 0 (5/5 targets typed, 40/40 guards typed, all shadow outputs ok). try_python/brevi_path confirm the reviewer's cases match CPython: fresh `{"ms": [], "err": 0}`, fresh `[0]`, `[[], 0]`, `([], 0)`, tuple unpacking inside loops, and `d["x"] is d["y"]` (dynamic, False). Shared-default case still goes dynamic."}
